WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.090
Being the 2nd born child in a Royal Family
can be a blessing in disguise.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:26.689
Nobody expects you to rule, so in a way you
are off the hook.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:32.430
But sometimes history gets in the way and
unexpected events can bring you in the limelight

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.732
and on the throne.

00:00:33.732 --> 00:00:38.230
That throne and that crown may represent one
of the youngest, smallest and most peaceful

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:43.570
nations in Europe, but that does not mean
that trouble won’t get in your way.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:48.530
This is what happened to today’s protagonist,
one of the longest serving and most respected

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:55.809
Royals of the 20th Century: King Haakon VII
of Norway, the voice of dignity against traitors

00:00:55.809 --> 00:00:58.520
and invaders.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:05.180
Prince Carl
At the time of King Haakon’s birth, Norway

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.710
was part of a Union with Sweden.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.030
It had an independent cabinet of ministers
and its own parliament, but the formal head

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:16.340
of State was the King of Norway and Sweden:
Carl IV, from the House of Bernadotte, based

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:17.490
in Stockholm.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.180
Therefore, Norway did not have a ruling dynasty
of its own.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.640
The man who would become the King of Norway
was actually a Danish Prince: born near Copenhagen

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.451
on 3 August 1872, in Charlottenlund Palace,
he was the second son of the Crown Prince

00:01:32.451 --> 00:01:35.820
Frederik and Crown Princess Louise of Denmark.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:42.240
At birth he was christened as Christian Frederik
Carl Georg Valdemar Axel, known simply as

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.110
‘Carl’ by those with shortness of breath.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:49.780
His elder brother would also become king in
the future, King Christian X of Denmark.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.470
While growing up, Carl would knock time and
again on Christian’s door asking

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.409
‘Do you wanna build a snowman?

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:57.340
Or ride our bikes around the halls?’

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.540
No, he didn’t.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.860
These guys were serious and received extensive
private tuition at the Palace.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:08.550
At the age of just 14, Prince Carl began his
training as a naval officer.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:14.470
He worked hard along the other cadets, and
was not accorded any special privilege.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.590
Carl graduated seven years later in 1893 with
the rank of sub-lieutenant of the Royal Danish

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.030
Navy, and was later promoted to first lieutenant.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:27.540
With that, Carl ticked one of the boxes expected
from a Prince: a military career.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.930
The second box was finding a suitable match,
in other words: meeting and marrying a girl

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.930
preferably from another European ruling house.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.440
At that time, you could not throw a stone
into a Royal Palace without hitting someone’s

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.000
cousin, due to the tight network of familiar
relationships amongst rulers.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.440
Carl did not stray from the norm and in 1896
he married his cousin from the United Kingdom:

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.959
she was Princess Maud, the daughter of King
Edward VII and his Danish wife, Queen Alexandra.

00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:58.580
Alexandra was the sister of Frederik, Carl’s
father.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:03.690
If this is getting confusing, here you can
feast your eyes with the family tree of the

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:08.730
Royal houses of Norway, Denmark, Sweden and
the United Kingdom, so you can have fun tracing

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.691
the blood ties amongst them:
I will post a link to the family tree in the

00:03:12.691 --> 00:03:15.710
description below, it’s got thumbnails and
all for every member.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.370
But let’s not forget about Maud.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.470
She was born in London on the 26th of November
1869.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:26.080
Since her early years, Princess Maud attended
regularly her mother’s family gatherings

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.080
in Denmark.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.520
It was in one of such occasions that she came
to know her cousin Carl.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:36.230
Their wedding was celebrated in Buckingham
Palace on the 22nd of July 1896, and the princely

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.500
couple settled in Copenhagen.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:44.310
But Carl and Maud always kept strong ties
with the UK, and in fact their son and only

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:50.270
child, Prince Alexander, was born at Appleton
House in Norfolk, England, on the 2nd of July

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.450
1903.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:56.050
Little did the young family know that their
lives were going to change drastically in

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.900
just a couple of years.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:06.319
Haakon
On 7 June 1905 the Norwegian parliament, known

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:11.250
as the ‘Storting’ passed a resolution
to dissolve the union with Sweden.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.070
The dissolution of the union between Norway
and Sweden was a momentous event which was

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:21.109
born from an apparently lesser dispute: a
conflict over the question of a separate Norwegian

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:22.620
consular service.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.930
Norway and Sweden had their own cabinets and
parliaments, but shared a single head of state,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:28.050
King Oscar II.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.970
But Norwegians always felt like the lesser
party in the union: in fact they did not have

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:38.110
their own foreign service missions, and were
subordinate to Sweden in all matters of foreign

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.110
policy.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:43.440
A new sense of national identity was brewing
in Norway and the Storting adopted a decision

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.779
to establish an independent Norwegian consular
service.

00:04:46.779 --> 00:04:50.860
For those not familiar with the subtleties
of diplomacy, Embassies maintain relationships

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:56.420
with the government and institutions of the
host country, while consulates support citizens

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.420
abroad.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:02.000
In practical terms, Norwegian expats and tourists
had to rely on Swedish consulates.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.969
King Oscar II refused to sanction the Storting’s
decision.

00:05:04.969 --> 00:05:07.360
As a result, the Norwegian Government resigned.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.220
The King did not succeed in appointing a new
government.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:14.970
This crisis meant that the union between the
two Scandinavian countries was no longer effective.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:20.920
And that takes us to the 7th of June 1905,
when the Storting assembly unilaterally voted

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.230
for a dissolution of the union.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:28.279
The Storting extended an offer to King Oscar
II to appoint a prince from his own House

00:05:28.279 --> 00:05:30.840
of Bernadotte as new King of Norway.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.860
This gesture of goodwill would have relieved
the tension now mounting between the two nations.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.789
King Oscar, though, formally declined the
offer.

00:05:38.789 --> 00:05:41.219
The Storting needed another candidate.

00:05:41.219 --> 00:05:46.210
An early version of LinkedIn would have tried
to solve the issue via a mass mailing to thousands

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.580
of unqualified applicants.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.700
But the Norwegian assembly was more effective
and turned to another candidate much closer

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:56.020
to home: our friend Prince Carl of Denmark.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:00.800
He clearly fitted the job description:
‘Job ID #1: Norwegian King and Head of State.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.330
The ideal candidate should fulfil the following
pre-requisites:

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.000
Male
Military officer, preferably in the Navy,

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.640
as they have the smartest uniforms
Scandinavian, familiar with Norwegian language

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.000
and culture
Related to the house of Bernadotte, for example

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.460
via his mother Louise, niece of King Oscar
II

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:23.020
With close ties with the most powerful nation
on Earth, the British Empire, for example

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.770
via marriage with a British Princess.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.589
Must have already sired a son, to ensure succession.

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:33.090
Clean driving licence
Good Excel and Powerpoint skills not essential

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:39.159
but welcomed’
The offer was extended to Carl in Autumn of

00:06:39.159 --> 00:06:40.159
that year.

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:44.660
The Danish Prince was not a power grabber
and had great consideration for the will of

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:45.660
the people.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:49.840
He was well-aware that the majority party
in the Storting, the Liberals, were considering

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.349
the idea of making Norway a Republic.

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:57.300
So, he insisted that before accepting the
crown, he should listen to the opinion of

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:03.410
the Norwegian people: on his proposal, it
was decided to hold a referendum on Norway’s

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:04.870
future form of government.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:09.000
The referendum was held on the 12th and 13th
of November 1905.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:15.140
The result was almost 260,000 votes in favour
of a monarchy versus almost 70,000 for the

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:16.140
republic.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.529
This gave Prince Carl a clear popular mandate.

00:07:19.529 --> 00:07:24.590
On the 18th of November, Carl found in his
inbox a telegram pinged by the President of

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:27.890
the Storting, formally offering him the Norwegian
throne.

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:32.770
Prince Carl happily accepted the offer, announcing
that he would change his name into Haakon,

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:38.210
while his son Alexander would be known as
Olav, due to his love of the summer season.

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:43.039
The King’s choice of name was significant:
in Old Norse, it translates as ‘High Son’

00:07:43.039 --> 00:07:47.460
or ‘Chosen Son’, and it had been used
by Norwegian kings centuries earlier, when

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.060
the Country was independent.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:54.909
Haakon also adopted a personal motto: ‘Alt
for Norge’ or ‘All for Norway’.

00:07:54.909 --> 00:08:01.500
A Chosen Son for an Independent Kingdom
On the 25th of November 1905 Norway’s new

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.580
royal family sailed into the capital Christiania
– later Oslo - on the naval vessel Heimdal,

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:09.200
named after the guardian God of Norse mythology.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:12.510
Prime Minister Michelsen welcomed the new
king with these words:

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:19.209
“It has been nearly 600 years since the
Norwegian people have had a king of their

00:08:19.209 --> 00:08:20.209
own.

00:08:20.209 --> 00:08:23.479
Not in all this time has he been solely our
own.

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:26.680
We have always had to share him with others
…

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:29.080
Chosen by a free people as a free man to lead
this country, he is to be our very own.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:33.820
Once again, the king of the Norwegian people
will emerge as a powerful, unifying symbol

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:39.260
of the new, independent Norway and all that
it shall undertake.”

00:08:39.260 --> 00:09:24.710
I so would love if this speech was followed
by knights chanting “The King

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:56.090
in

00:09:56.090 --> 00:10:08.250
The North!

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:31.080
The King 
in

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:32.440
the North!”

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:36.580
But it didn’t.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:43.310
These were serious guys.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:50.070
The speech was followed by a salvo of cannons
and church bells ringing throughout Oslo.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:54.050
On the 27th King Haakon swore an oath of allegiance
to the constitution.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:11:00.210
On 22 June 1906, King Haakon and Queen Maud
were crowned in Nidaros Cathedral in Trondheim.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:04.571
Once again Haakon showed to be a king of the
people: well-aware that the custom of the

00:11:04.571 --> 00:11:09.710
coronation was considered to be archaic and
undemocratic by many citizens, he opted for

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:10.920
a subdued ceremony.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:16.380
For instance, the traditional lavish procession
previously staged by the Bernadottes was eliminated:

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:21.140
the King and Queen were escorted to the cathedral
in a single coach drawn by four horses.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:25.700
The travel itself to Trondheim was conducted
with little ceremony by train and carriages,

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:30.130
with many stops along the way so that Haakon
and Maud could get acquainted with the people

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:31.410
of their new country.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.890
Once they arrived at Nidaros Cathedral they
were welcomed by 2,300 attendees and by the

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:41.120
two Bishops who were to perform the ceremony:
Bishop Wexelsen of Nidaros, and the wonderfully

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.480
named Bishop Bang of Oslo.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:46.960
That was his real surname, look it up.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.600
Bishop Bang greeted the King with the words:
“May the Lord bless your going in and your

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.280
coming out now and for evermore.”

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.260
Very appropriate for Bishop Bang.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:59.540
I will stop there before I am sent to Hell.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:03.890
Immediately after the coronation King Haakon
immersed himself in Norwegian politics and

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:04.890
culture.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:10.140
Polar explorer Fridtjof Nansen introduced
the Royals to the national sport: skiing.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:15.330
King Haakon also started new traditions, such
as the much-loved annual children’s parade.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:20.650
But besides these rare popular celebrations,
he and Maud showed great moderation, especially

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:23.190
in comparison with other royal families of
the time.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:26.280
The Royal Court was small and expenses were
kept to a minimum.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:29.310
This was yet another sign of Haakon’s political
sensibility.

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:34.170
Well-aware that more than 20% of the nation
had republican sympathies he felt it important

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.890
to maintain a modest lifestyle.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:40.850
This moderation was also in line with Norwegian
tradition and respected the fact that Norway

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:43.320
was not a wealthy country at that time.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:47.830
Today the country is the 8th largest producer
of oil worldwide and 3rd largest producer

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:51.780
of gas, but the first drillings were still
decades away.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:57.140
King Haakon was also wary of respecting the
Norwegian constitution, to which he had sworn

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:58.520
an oath of allegiance.

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:03.260
He firmly believed that political power should
be in the hands of democratically elected

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:07.330
representatives, but he still wished to be
kept closely informed about political affairs

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:08.440
by the Government.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:12.930
Although he might state his views on a certain
case, he always deferred to the majority in

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:18.730
the Council of State, unfailingly supported
policy decisions, and was careful never to

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:21.779
show an affinity for any political party.

00:13:21.779 --> 00:13:26.160
During his first years on the throne, Haakon
oversaw important reforms, such as the institution

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.850
of universal female suffrage in 1913.

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:35.340
But a testing ordeal was just round the corner:
WWI.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:43.330
The Neutral Ally
In the years preceding WWI, Haakon’s Foreign

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:49.210
Minister Jørgen Løvland outlined Norwegian
foreign along two directives: neutrality,

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.600
in combination with an active trade policy.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:56.060
Neutrality eschewed political alliances that
might drag the country in foreign wars, with

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.800
the implicit agreement that Norway was within
the British sphere of influence.

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:03.840
The government expected the British Empire
to defend the young country in case of foreign

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:04.840
aggression.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:09.540
When Germany declared war on Russia on 1 August,
Norway, along with Sweden and Denmark, issued

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.380
a declaration of neutrality.

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:16.110
The next priority for the country was to provide
supplies, feed the population and maintain

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:17.650
economic stability.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:22.089
This led to a difficult balancing act for
the Norwegian government: on the one hand,

00:14:22.089 --> 00:14:26.490
trade with Germany was vital for the country’s
economy; on the other hand, the UK pressured

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:30.150
Norway to become a part of the economic blockade
of Germany.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:36.200
In the spring and summer of 1915 Germany started
to buy huge quantities of one particular supply

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:38.750
from Norway: fish.

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:42.850
Tons of fish, any fish related item they could
get hold of.

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:43.850
The result?

00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:45.560
There was hardly any fish left for Norwegians.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:46.950
Cod was out of stock!

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:48.240
Salmon was out of stock!

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:49.240
Haddock was out of stock!

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:51.300
Stock fish was out of stock!

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.300
[Caption: The Irony!]

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:53.300
Fish stocks were out of fish!

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:54.300
I think I made my point.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:55.300
What little fish was available was incredibly
expensive.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:59.580
In an attempt to quell exports to Germany,
Britain decided they would buy Norwegian fish

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:00.580
instead.

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.930
This decision decreased even more the availability
and affordability of a vital food supply,

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:08.920
while flooding Britain with stocks of Norwegian
stock fish.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.420
In Autumn 1916 life was dire for Norwegians.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:16.740
To continue with the fishing theme, they were
caught between a rock and a hard plaice.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:20.870
On one side, the German Navy had extended
their unrestricted submarine warfare to the

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:25.420
Arctic Sea, sinking several Norwegian merchant
ships trading with Russia.

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:30.220
On the other side, Great Britain had imposed
an embargo on coal imports, as leverage to

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.200
prevent Norway from selling copper ore to
Germany.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:36.500
Haakon and Maud were not oblivious to the
plight of their people.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:40.420
In addition to conducting an even more frugal
lifestyle, the Royal Couple established a

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:44.400
fund to assist citizens in extremely difficult
circumstances.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:48.640
Luckily for the country, the Norwegian and
British governments reached a deal, the ‘Tonnage

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:51.130
Agreement’ in February 1917.

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:56.070
This was a very clever set-up: first, Britain
would lift the coal embargo; in exchange,

00:15:56.070 --> 00:16:00.310
Norway would suspend trade with Germany and
supply the Entente instead.

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:04.290
The Norwegian merchant ships would travel
in convoys protected by the Royal Navy, reducing

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:07.260
the risk of sinking by the Kaiser’s submarines.

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:11.300
The clever bit was that the agreement was
signed by the Norwegian Ship Owners Association,

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:16.440
which camouflaged the Norwegian Government’s
involvement allowing to preserve a formal

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:17.440
neutrality.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.070
Hence, Norway became known as ‘the neutral
ally’.

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:24.350
The positive effects of this agreement, though,
did not reach all levels of society immediately.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:29.610
In June 1917, ordinary citizens were still
struggling to cope with a cost of living that

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:33.500
had increased by 250% since the start of the
war.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:37.870
Over 300,000 people took to the streets in
June to demonstrate against a lack of food

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:42.940
and money to pay for necessities – roughly
one seventh of the overall population!

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:47.300
The government did not fall, although this
crisis led to a radicalisation of the Labour

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:53.130
Party, whose members even considered revolution
as an answer.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:58.940
Between Two Wars
Luckily for Haakon, the revolution did not

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:06.620
take place, rather an evolution of the electoral
system in 1919 which ensured a fairer representation

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:08.110
of the working classes.

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:12.510
Years later, in 1927, this reform led the
Labour Party to winning the majority at the

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:13.510
Storting.

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:18.089
In 1928, Haakon resisted pressures to form
a government led by the conservative ‘Agrarian

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:22.600
Party’ and respected Labour’s majority
by appointing its leader Christopher Hornsrud

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:23.600
as Prime Minister.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:27.740
The conservatives were not happy about this
decision, but it would take only three years

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:29.890
for the Agrarian Party to return to power.

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:34.830
Amongst their ranks, a former military attaché,
now Defense Minister, became very active in

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:40.610
using the armed forces to quell union strikes
and keep the spectre of Bolshevism at bay.

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:42.550
His name was Vidkun Quisling.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:45.760
I warmly invite you to watch our Biographics
episode about Vidkun Quisling, to learn more

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:51.059
about how he became the Nazi collaborator
by definition, a traitor of his own country,

00:17:51.059 --> 00:17:53.990
the man who welcomed an invasion so he could
be Prime Minister.

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:54.990
Or
‘The Man Who Sold the Fjord’

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:55.990
if you like.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:58.650
As a quick summary: Former Defense Minister
Quisling founded the National Union party

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:03.170
in May 1933, modelling it after the Nazi and
Fascist parties.

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:07.179
Defeated in two consecutive elections, Quisling
forged a friendship with Hitler’s advisor

00:18:07.179 --> 00:18:11.980
Alfred Rosenberg and exploited these ties
to facilitate the German occupation of Norway.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:17.000
But during the 1930’s these schemes had
not yet become a source of worry for King

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.530
Haakon, he had to face more personal issues.

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:24.780
On the 20th of November 1938, his beloved
Queen Maud died in London after a prolonged

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:27.120
illness, while visiting her British relatives.

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:32.490
Maud was buried in the Royal Mausoleum at
Akershus Castle.

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:37.020
Apparently shy in public, Maud was known for
being warm and vivacious with family and friends.

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:41.170
Her original personality set her apart from
other Queens of the time: she enjoyed outdoor

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:46.800
activities like riding or skiing, more than
court protocol, was a keen dancer and a competent

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:50.510
photographer, and was directly involved in
Prince Olav’s upbringing.

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:56.159
Haakon would surely have loved to have her
by his side during his – and his country’s

00:18:56.159 --> 00:18:59.440
– darkest hour: the German invasion of April
1940.

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:02.760
The Voice of the Resistance
In our Quisling video we cover in detail the

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:06.990
invasion of Norway, the country’s resistance
efforts and its eventual liberation.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:11.320
Today I will talk about the same events, but
from Haakon’s perspective.

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:15.670
An occupation of Norway was part of the III
Reich’s strategy, as the country could provide

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:19.270
the German war effort with iron ore and an
extensive merchant navy.

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:23.800
It would have happened regardless of Quisling’s
scheming to facilitate the German invasion,

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:25.980
hoping to be installed as Prime Minister.

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.810
German troops invaded Norway on 9 April 1940.

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:34.080
The heavy cruiser Blücher sailed into the
Oslo fjord in the early morning hours of that

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:35.970
day, transporting a landing force.

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:40.860
Their task was to arrest the King and the
members of the Government to compel Norway

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:41.970
to capitulate immediately.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:44.390
But the Norwegians were no pushovers.

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:48.750
The Fjord was protected by the cannons of
the Oscarsborg Fortress, as well as the torpedo

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:49.870
batteries in Kaholmen Island.

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:56.340
They all opened fire on the Blücher and she
sank at 06:22am with 1,300 sailors on board.

00:19:56.340 --> 00:20:00.260
The sinking of the Blücher delayed the German
troops’ advance on Oslo, giving the Royal

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:06.220
Family, the Government and the Storting representatives
the time needed to escape to safety in Elverum,

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:07.220
Eastern Norway.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:11.870
There, the Storting gave the King and the
Government full authority to rule the country

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:13.230
for the duration of the war.

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:17.940
On the same day, Crown Prince Olav ensured
the safety of his family, by having his wife

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:21.040
Märtha and their three children cross the
border to Sweden.

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:23.520
They then travelled to the US in August.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:27.390
Thanks to Martha’s friendship with Franklin
and Eleanor Roosevelt, she became a sort of

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:30.120
unofficial Norwegian Ambassador in Washington.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:34.420
On the 10th of April King Haakon met with
the German envoy, Curt Bräuer.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:36.890
The Germans demanded for the Government headed
by Johan Nygaardsvold to step down.

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:41.210
The King had to appoint a new government headed
by Vidkun Quisling.

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:45.230
The King put forth the German demands in an
extraordinary meeting of the Council of State

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:46.840
in the village of Nybergsund.

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:50.580
Haakon stated that he would not attempt to
influence the decision of the Government in

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:54.080
this matter, but that he could not comply
with the German ultimatum.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:57.950
He would rather abdicate than have Quisling
as Prime Minister.

00:20:57.950 --> 00:21:01.350
Without directly imposing his will, the King
had managed to influence the decision of his

00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:03.440
ministers into rejecting Quisling.

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:07.430
Upon learning of the King’s refusal, German
forces repeatedly bombed Nybergsund, but he

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:09.220
and Olav managed to escape to safety.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:13.280
On the 15th of April, the first Allied landing
party had arrived in Narvik.

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:17.910
A combined Anglo-French-Polish force would
support the Norwegian Army in engaging the

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:18.910
German troops.

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:23.140
But the Allies were ill-prepared for mountain
warfare and the Luftwaffe had complete air

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:24.140
superiority.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:28.070
By the end of May, the British cabinet had
decided for a total retreat to avoid losing

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:30.660
the whole expeditionary force to the Germans.

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:32.820
King Haakon was left with a difficult choice.

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:34.350
Fleeing or staying?

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:38.660
Staying by his people would have seemed natural,
but the Germans were not hiding their intentions

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:39.660
to have him captured.

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:42.990
He could have been forced to provide legitimacy
to Quisling’s government.

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:46.730
In the end he decided to flee the Country:
he would establish a Government in exile in

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:51.520
London, from where he could denounce Quisling
or any other collaborationist cabinet, and

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:53.020
lead the resistance effort.

00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:56.742
On 7 June 1940 the King, the Crown Prince
and the Government travelled from Tromsø

00:21:56.742 --> 00:21:57.742
to England.

00:21:57.742 --> 00:21:59.330
On the 9th, Norway had surrendered.

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:04.030
The Norwegians and their allies held out for
two months against a German invasion, longer

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:07.090
than any other country except for the Soviet
Union.

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:09.600
As I said: no pushovers.

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:13.940
From London, King Haakon became the foremost
symbol of the Norwegian people’s will to

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:18.640
fight for a free and independent Norway, and
his radio broadcasts from London served as

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:21.840
a source of inspiration for young and old
alike.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:25.600
Back in Norway, the Germans had realised that
Quisling was an unreliable leader with little

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:26.770
popular support.

00:22:26.770 --> 00:22:32.070
The reins of the country were effectively
taken by Reichskommissar Josef Terboven.

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:37.610
He attempted to establish a legal occupation
government, elected by the Storting, to collaborate

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:38.610
with the Nazis.

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:40.910
But this required the King’s abdication.

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:46.150
In a speech on the 8th of July 1940 Haakon
once again made clear that he would not give

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:51.520
in to German demands, and that he was still
the legitimate head of the Norwegian state.

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:55.670
On the 25th of September Terboven abandoned
all plans to collaborate with the existing

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:56.929
Norwegian authorities.

00:22:56.929 --> 00:23:02.120
He declared the King and the Government deposed
and outlawed all political parties, except

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:03.860
for Quisling’s National Union.

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:09.120
All activities in support of the Royal Family
were forbidden, but King Haakon and the government-in-exile

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:13.280
stood firm in their resolve to fight until
Norway was liberated.

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:18.170
Over the next five years, Haakon’s speeches
would inspire daily, constant acts of civil

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:20.559
disobedience against the German occupiers.

00:23:20.559 --> 00:23:25.100
Haakon’s monogram, a ‘7’ imposed on
a capital ‘H’ became a symbol of defiance.

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:29.299
Haakon also inspired the creation of the Milorg
– or Home Front.

00:23:29.299 --> 00:23:33.970
This resistance movement was a constant thorn
in the Nazis’ side: it worked closely with

00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:38.700
the Special Operations Executive and was responsible
for the destruction of the Telemark plant,

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:41.820
which stalled German research on the Atomic
bomb.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:47.110
By the end of the War, Norway was garrisoned
by approximately 400,000 German troops, 1

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:49.030
soldier for every 6 citizens.

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:53.390
This gives an indication of how difficult
it was for the Reich to tame the Norwegians.

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:55.370
Did I mention that these guys were no pushovers?

00:23:55.370 --> 00:24:05.820
Return of the Chosen Son
Germany capitulated on 8 May 1945.

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:10.510
Terboven committed suicide on the same day,
while Quisling was trialled and executed in

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:11.750
October.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:16.570
King Haakon returned home on the 7th of June,
the fifth anniversary of his escape to London.

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:21.110
In late summer 1945 he embarked on a tour
of the country to see for himself the destruction

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:26.320
caused by the war, as well as to encourage
the ongoing efforts to rebuild infrastructure.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:29.980
The Norwegians were happy to have their King
back, and grateful for his resolve during

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:31.340
the ordeal of the War.

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:36.490
A collection was launched to raise funds for
a special 75th birthday present: A Royal Yacht.

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:41.460
King Haakon accepted this gift in 1947 and
re-christened the vessel the Norge, which

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:43.780
he used in many official visits abroad.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:48.179
Haakon continued to maintain strong ties with
the UK, even after Maud’s death.

00:24:48.179 --> 00:24:51.630
During one of his visits to London, he attended
a theatrical performance of ‘The Moon is

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:53.490
Down’, by John Steinbeck.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:59.110
The original novel was published in 1942 as
a means to encourage resistance in Nazi occupied

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:03.840
countries, and its setting, characters and
plot were based on the invasion of Norway.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:08.620
One of the protagonists is the elderly, calm,
authoritative Mayor Orden, who encourages

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:12.960
his citizens to disobedience and open rebellion
against the invaders.

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:17.980
It is not difficult to find Haakon’s influence
on this character and on his opposition to

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:21.540
the treasonous shop keeper Corell, a Quisling
in disguise.

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:27.690
After 52 years on the throne, aged 85, King
Haakon VII died at the Royal Palace in Oslo

00:25:27.690 --> 00:25:33.620
on the 21st of September 1957, and was buried
at Maud’s side, in the Royal Mausoleum at

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:34.620
Akershus Castle.

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:37.530
I hope you enjoyed today’s Biographics.

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:42.309
As a final thought, I will leave you with
Haakon’s dilemma: would you have stayed

00:25:42.309 --> 00:25:49.010
in Norway, by your people, and risk giving
legitimacy – even involuntarily – to Quisling

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:50.010
and Terboven?

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:52.100
Or would you flee the country like he did?

00:25:52.100 --> 00:25:56.700
And a small quiz for you: can you name an
European royal who decided to stay after the

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:57.910
German invasion?

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:06.340
Post your answers and opinion in the comments,
and as usual … thank you for watching!

